    "responsePreview": "Here's a straightforward version, assuming an `orders` table with `customer_id` and an order timestamp column:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    o.order_id,\n    o.customer_id,\n    o.order_date,\n    o.status,\n    o.total_amount\nFROM orders AS o\nWHERE o.customer_id = :customer_id\nORDER BY o.order_date DESC, o.order_id DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\nNotes:\n\n- `:customer_id` is a bind parameter; replace it with your driver's placeholder (`?`, `$1`, `@customer_id`, etc.) rather than interpolating the value into the string.\n- The secondary sort on `order_id` makes the result deterministic when two orders share the same timestamp.\n- `LIMIT 10` works in PostgreSQL, MySQL, and SQLite. For SQL Server use `SELECT TOP 10 ...` (and drop the `LIMIT`), or for standard SQL / Oracle 12c+ use `FETCH FIRST 10 ROWS ONLY` in place of `LIMIT 10`.\n- An index on `(customer_id, order_date DESC)` will make this query efficient for customers with many orders.\n\nIf you share your actual schema (table and column names), I can tailor the query to match.",
